>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Colour blindness closes a few doors in India, such as some pilot, armed forces, railway and
    merchant navy posts, and leaves most careers open, so the first job is to get a proper test and
    read each recruiter's own standard.</strong> Courts have already opened film courses, medical study
    and an engineer's job to colour-blind people. The decisions that unlock high income opportunities
    then come from choosing colour-light work and building a high-income skill portfolio.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Test first, then choose.</strong> Get a written result from an eye doctor before you pay for coaching. Armed forces, aviation, railways and merchant navy run colour tests, and each sets its own standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Most careers stay open.</strong> Software, data, accessible design, film editing, finance, law, teaching and many engineering roles involve little colour-critical work.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> list your two target routes, check each one's medical standard, then finish one real project. It is the start of a high-income skill portfolio and a quicker route to financial freedom.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide is part of our set of
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options for every stream and degree</a>. Rights, quotas and
    scholarships for listed disabilities are in
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-students-with-disabilities-india/">career options for students with disabilities</a>.
    If you are choosing a stream, start with
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th/">career options after 12th</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="get-tested">Get a proper test before you choose</h2>

  <p>
    A school screening is a hint, not a diagnosis. Colour vision deficiency comes in types and degrees,
    and recruiters treat a mild result and a strong one differently.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Test</th>
          <th>What it is</th>
          <th>How to use it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {testRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Test"><strong>{row.test}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it is">{row.what}</td>
            <td data-label="How to use it">{row.use}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Ask an eye specialist for a written report that names the type and degree. Keep it with your
    certificates, and take it with you when you read any recruiter's medical standard.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law">Is colour blindness a disability in law?</h2>

  <p>
    Usually not in the sense of a quota. In October 2023 the Supreme Court noted in the TANGEDCO case
    that the engineer did not fit the RPwD Act's categories and did not meet the 40 percent benchmark.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Court still held that the employer had to consider reasonable accommodation, meaning
    appropriate modification and adjustments that do not impose a disproportionate or undue burden.
    So there is no dedicated seat or post reservation for colour vision alone, but there is a duty to
    adjust where it is reasonable.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The law will not open a post that a safety regulator has closed on medical grounds. It does push
      employers to justify a blanket ban with a stated rule and expert material, as the TANGEDCO ruling
      shows. Plan for both: some doors will stay shut, and others are shut only by habit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-tests">Who tests colour vision, and how</h2>

  <p>
    This table records what the documents we read say. Standards change, so use it to ask the right
    question and then read the recruiter's current notice.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where</th>
          <th>What the document says</th>
          <th>What to do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
